#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a1706a-4673-5227-9b96-0f43f57c2654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a1706a-4673-5227-9b96-0f43f57c2654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363148} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f4f302-020e-5b73-b482-25244b345700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6de9c5fb-6602-5ae1-aa69-c55326fc24ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-314898" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/d27b0d70171683240fd44c8bbbcc8cb46ce0f0f216533a8061d31a991cce4e14.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=1$ and $x=3.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+1)-f(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c64c70-ed7a-5b61-b68d-56bde8cdf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363151} {:answer/id #uuid "abeeed9f-7672-5c5d-b5a4-171154207120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abeeed9f-7672-5c5d-b5a4-171154207120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363152} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52efb1e-62b6-5718-a3a2-015dafb2fbcd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a9d5dd-0ec2-50b8-9366-60a007d05b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-344023" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/5663e7c59a9f42b31ebadd9abb54e36c604526c00334f840014a674c5b6dcdb8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=3.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=3$ and $x=9.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$ with step size $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{f(6+3)-f(6-3)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(9)-f(3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0851df65-bd54-5434-a1cc-e96b434d83be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0851df65-bd54-5434-a1cc-e96b434d83be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363155} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a6ae30-4111-5512-8a8e-971e89796c78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e7f6591-29fc-5c59-a67f-cf600089ba65" :question/math-academy-id "q-344346" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/068497b2e879c756fbf8a73942e985fe9a631c5c34f7e069138f8eb99fdd1692.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=3.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=3$ and $x=9.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$ with step size $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{f(6+3)-f(6-3)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(9)-f(3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-7)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f723b9a0-fde8-59ce-af43-700fcc2e1b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363158} {:answer/id #uuid "61133538-ed94-51ba-8070-9d2af7b8af69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363159} {:answer/id #uuid "2d02597e-7803-55c5-8238-606dc772af76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{liter}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363160} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd95bc9-2c67-5423-970d-2fd3ccb4daa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363161} {:answer/id #uuid "70f8ee91-5913-52b5-b2d0-e23b0de04905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f723b9a0-fde8-59ce-af43-700fcc2e1b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363158} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec118a65-c1ef-50ae-8986-b2405e65d71f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4f25da8-2ff6-5beb-8f04-c8b7f4871db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47361" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/fa3045b53d5a91ae7adbf450243338b988239749e4ae7340938943cce4c239fb.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2+1)-q(2-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(3)-q(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2}{2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of leaking at the instant of time $t=2$ is $0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c401e6a-491e-5879-9c26-6cdbcda13b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363165} {:answer/id #uuid "c4790b77-decf-5926-9cb2-58182b9be430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363166} {:answer/id #uuid "a09396f7-c099-5195-b4c1-6289d1bf813e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363167} {:answer/id #uuid "b715c8e2-2c34-529d-95df-0a1717fe8c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168} {:answer/id #uuid "692d4f65-0229-5764-b067-de8f938a66eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b715c8e2-2c34-529d-95df-0a1717fe8c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a002b2b-fab3-559e-aecb-b50db807f989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbb323c7-ac71-5633-b844-f3d6a3159331" :question/math-academy-id "q-51405" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/1e11260301be8c0bb19a9fd47ffbbf71cc5fa4d94dbeae57af934b76fb17f0e1.png)\n\nA particle has a speed of $v(t)$ meters per second after $t$ seconds. The graph of $v(t)$ is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of speed when $t=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{v}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{v(t+h)-g(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{v(3+1)-v(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(4)-v(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of speed at the instant of time $t=2$ is approximately $1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"}]}
